   <div class=\"metadata-item\">\n                <span class=\"metadata-label\">Submitted:</span> 19 May 2023\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"metadata-item\">\n                <span class=\"metadata-label\">Last Updated:</span> 19 May 2023\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"metadata-item\">\n                <span class=\"metadata-label\">Categories:</span> <span class=\"category\">cs.CL</span>, <span class=\"category\">cs.AI</span>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class=\"actions\">\n            <a href=\"http://127.0.0.1:8034/pdf/2305.12345\" class=\"btn\">Download PDF</a>\n            <a href=\"/html/search?q=DecodingTrust:\" class=\"btn btn-secondary\">Find Similar</a>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class=\"abstract\">\n            <h2>Abstract</h2>\n            <div class=\"abstract-content\">\n                Large language models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across various domains, raising concerns about their trustworthiness. In this work, we present DecodingTrust, a comprehensive framework for evaluating the trustworthiness of Generative Pre-trained Transformers (GPT models). We introduce a multi-dimensional assessment that encompasses safety, fairness, transparency, robustness, and alignment. Our evaluation covers a diverse suite of benchmarks including toxic language detection, biased reasoning, privacy leakage, and adversarial vulnerability. Through extensive experiments on various GPT model variants, we identify critical vulnerabilities and provide actionable insights for improving model trustworthiness. Our findings reveal that while larger models show improved performance on many metrics, they also exhibit new failure modes that require careful attention. We release our evaluation toolkit to the community to facilitate ongoing research in trustworthy AI.\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n",
